  {
    "content" : "Based on my exploration of the project, I can now explain how it's organized:\n\n## Project Structure and Components\n\n### Main Parts\n\n1. **CLI Interface** (`cli.py`)\n   - Provides command-line interface for inventory operations\n   - Supports commands: `add`, `list`, and `reserve`\n   - Uses `SqliteStore` for data persistence\n   - Interacts with `Item` model objects\n\n2. **Inventory Module** (`inventory\/`)\n   - Contains core business logic and data models\n\n### Key Modules and Classes\n\n**Models** (`inventory\/models.py`):\n- `Item`: Data class representing inventory items with SKU, name, and quantity\n- `Reservation`: Data class for tracking stock reservations\n\n**Storage** (`inventory\/storage.py`):\n- `SqliteStore`: SQLite database backend for inventory management\n- `InsufficientStock`: Exception raised when trying to reserve more stock than available\n- Methods: `add_item()`, `get_item()`, `list_items()`, `reserve_stock()`, `release()`\n\n**API Layer** (`inventory\/api.py`):\n- HTTP handlers for inventory operations\n- `list_items()`: Returns all inventory items\n- `reserve()`: Handles stock reservation with error handling\n- Depends on `SqliteStore` and `price_with_tax()` from pricing module\n\n**Pricing** (`inventory\/pricing.py`):\n- Implements pricing logic with tax and volume discounts\n- `unit_discount()`: Calculates discount based on quantity thresholds\n- `price_with_tax()`: Computes final price including tax\n\n**Tests** (`tests\/test_storage.py`):\n- Unit tests for storage functionality\n- Tests stock reservation and insufficient stock scenarios\n\n### How They Connect\n\n1. The CLI (`cli.py`) is the main entry point that uses `SqliteStore` from `inventory.storage`\n2. `SqliteStore` manages SQLite database operations and interacts with `Item` and `Reservation` models\n3. The API layer (`api.py`) provides HTTP handlers that also use `SqliteStore`\n4. Pricing calculations are used in the API layer for reservation responses\n5. All components share the same data models defined in `models.py`\n\nThe system follows a layered architecture where the CLI and API both interact with the same underlying storage layer, ensuring consistency between different interfaces to the inventory system.",
